From: mothershipOS
Subject: [PATCH] media: venus: run sessions whose estimated load exceeds the highest clock

The core selection refused a new session (STREAMON EINVAL, "HW can't support this load") when the summed
estimate (macroblocks x declared fps x cycles per macroblock) exceeded the highest clock. The estimate comes
from what clients declare, and refusing breaks clients that open several sessions: Firefox in the Ubuntu VM
(virtio-media) kept a 2160p VP9 session at a declared 60 fps (388 of 444 MHz) and every further decoder
(ad, next video, quality switch) was refused, so YouTube videos never started. Run the session instead, the
core at its highest clock (load_scale_v4 clamps there); real overload only slows decoding.

---
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/pm_helpers.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/pm_helpers.c
@@ -688,8 +688,15 @@
 		cu.video_core_enable_mask = min_lp_coreid;
 		move_core_to_power_save_mode(core, min_lp_coreid);
 	} else {
-		dev_warn(core->dev, "HW can't support this load");
-		return -EINVAL;
+		/*
+		 * The estimate exceeds the highest clock: run anyway, the core at its highest clock
+		 * (load_scale_v4 clamps). The load comes from what clients declare (S_PARM), not from
+		 * what they decode, and refusing a session breaks clients that open several (a
+		 * browser's ad and video, the next video preloaded); real overload only slows them.
+		 */
+		dev_warn_ratelimited(core->dev, "load above the highest clock: running at it\n");
+		inst->clk_data.core_id = min_coreid;
+		cu.video_core_enable_mask = min_coreid;
 	}
 
 done:
